          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#appearance_in_a_model_structure Appearance in a model structure
          %p
            There is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+model+category"} Quillen model category
            structure on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
            where fibrations are Hurewicz fibrations, cofibrations are closed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hurewicz+cofibrations"} Hurewicz cofibrations
            and weak equivalences are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+equivalences"} homotopy equivalences
            – see at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+topological+spaces"} model structure on topological spaces
            and specifically at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Str%C3%B8m%27s+model+category"} Strøm's model category
          %p
            There is a version of Hurewicz fibrations for
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+spaces"} pointed spaces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/slice+category"} slice category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Top
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %msub
                    %mi B
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Top/B_0
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi B
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B_0
            is a fixed base (the
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/slice+model+structure"} slice model structure
          %p
            There is also a model category whose fibrations are the Hurewicz fibrations and whose weak equivalences are the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+homotopy+equivalences"} weak homotopy equivalences
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mixed+model+structure"} mixing
            the above model structure with the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+model+structure+on+topological+spaces"} classical model structure on topological spaces
          %h3#relation_to_serre_fibrations Relation to Serre fibrations
          %p
            Every Hurewicz fibration is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Serre+fibration"} Serre fibration
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/a+Serre+fibration+between+CW-complexes+is+a+Hurewicz+fibration"} a Serre fibration between CW-complexes is a Hurewicz fibration
          %h3#LocalRecognition Local recognition over numerable open covers
          %p
            The following proposition says that being a Hurewicz fibration is a
            %em local property
            with respect to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/codomain"} codomain
            space, at least as long as the local
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+cover"} open cover
            used is
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/numerable+open+cover"} numerable
          %p
          #MapIsHurewiczFibrationIfPullbackToNumerableCoverIsSo.num_prop
            %h6 Proposition
            %p
              %strong (Local recognition of Hurewicz fibrations over numerable open covers)
              %br/
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi E
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \colon E \to B
              be a continuous function such that there exists a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/numerable+open+cover"} numerable open cover
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mover
                      %mo ⟶
                      %mi ϕ
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \overset{\phi}{\longrightarrow} B
              over which
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
              is a Hurewicz fibration, hence such that the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo *
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi^\ast p
              is a Hurewicz fibration.
            %p
              Then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
              is a Hurewicz fibration
            %p
          %p
            A proof may be found spelled out in e.g.
            %a{:href => "#May99"} May 99, Sec. 7.4
          %h2#Abstractly Abstract Hurewicz fibrations
          %p
            The concept of Hurewicz fibrations makes sense also more generally in the presence of a (well behaved)
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interval+object"} interval object
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Kamps72"} Kamps 72
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Williamson13"} Williamson 13
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            is in (
            %a{:href => "#Warren08"}> Warren 08
            ).
          %h2#Examples Examples
          %h3#empty_bundles Empty bundles
          %p
          #EmptyBundlesAreHurewiczFibrations.num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+bundles"} empty bundles
                are
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hurewicz+fibrations"} Hurewicz fibrations
              %br/
              All
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+bundles"} empty bundles
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∅
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varnothing \longrightarrow B
              are Hurewicz fibrations, because none of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commuting+squares"} commuting squares
              that one would have to non-trivially
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lifting+property"} lift in
              actually exist:
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo ⟶
                              %mrow
                                %mo ¬
                                %mo ∃
                          %mtd
                            %mo ∅
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} ↓
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                            %mo ×
                            %mi I
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⟶
                          %mtd
                            %mi B
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                                %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    X &amp;\overset{ \not \exists }{\longrightarrow}&amp; \varnothing
                    \\\
                    \\big\downarrow &amp;&amp; \big\downarrow
                    \\\
                    X \times I &amp;\longrightarrow&amp; B
                    \\mathrlap{\,,}
                    }
            %p
              since the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+topological+space"} empty topological space
              is a
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+initial+object"} strict initial object
              %em no
              morphism to it from any
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inhabited+space"} inhabited space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %p
              (If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              itself is
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+topological+space"} empty
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \times I
              and then a square only exists if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
              in turn is empty. This square, consisting entirely of empty spaces, does have a unique lift, trivially, and hence also the empty bundle over the empty space is a Hurewicz fibration – for what it’s worth.)
            %p
          %h3#covering_spaces Covering spaces
          #CoveringSpaceIsHurewiczFibration.num_example
            %h6#example Example
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covering+spaces"} covering spaces
                are Hurewicz fibrations)
            %p
              Every
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covering+space"} covering space
              projection is a Hurewicz fibration, by
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "covering#space#HomotopyLiftingPropertyOfCoveringSpaces"} this prop.
          %h3#FiberBundles Fiber bundles
          %p
          #NumerableFiberBundlesAreSerreFibrations.num_prop
            %h6 Proposition
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/numerable+fiber+bundles"} numerable fiber bundles
                are Hurewicz fibrations)
              %br/
            %p
              Every
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/numerable+fiber+bundle"} numerable fiber bundle
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+bundle"} fiber bundle
              over a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/paracompact+topological+space"} paracompact topological space
            %p
          .proof
            %h6 Proof
            %p
              By definition of local triviality, for a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/numerable+fiber+bundle"} numerable fiber bundle
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi E
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \colon E \to B
              – such as a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+bundle"} fiber bundle
              over a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/paracompact+topological+space"} paracompact topological space
              – there exists a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/numerable+open+cover"} numerable open cover
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi U
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi \colon U \to B
              such that the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback+bundle"} pullback bundle
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo *
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi^\ast p
              is trivial, i.e. is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+product"} Cartesian product
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projection"} projection
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo *
                    %mi p
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi U
                    %mo ×
                    %mi F
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\phi^\ast p \;\colon\; U \times F \longrightarrow U
            %p
              (for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              the
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/typical+fiber"} typical fiber
              = succeed "." do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#MapIsHurewiczFibrationIfPullbackToNumerableCoverIsSo"}
          %p
            Notice that the example of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+bundles"} empty bundles
            (Example
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#EmptyBundlesAreHurewiczFibrations"}
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+bundle"} fiber bundle
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/typical+fiber"} typical fiber
            the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+topological+space"} empty topological space
          %h2#related_concept Related concept
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hurewicz+cofibration"} Hurewicz cofibration
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Str%C3%B8m+model+structure"} Strøm model structure
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Serre+fibration"} Serre fibration
          %h2#references References
          %p The original article:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Witold+Hurewicz"}> Witold Hurewicz
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em On the concept of fiber space
              %strong 41
              (1955) 956–961; (
              %a{:href => "https://dx.doi.org/10.1073%2Fpnas.41.11.956"}> doi:10.1073%2Fpnas.41.11.956
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://www.jstor.org/stable/89187"} jstor:89187
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "http://www.pnas.org/content/41/11/956.full.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            Review of Hurewicz fibrations,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hurewicz+connections"} Hurewicz connections
            and related issues:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/James+Eells"}> James Eells
              , Jr.:
              = succeed "," do
                %em Fibring spaces of maps
              = succeed "," do
                %em Symposium on infinite-dimensional topology
              = succeed "," do
                %strong 69
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://press.princeton.edu/books/paperback/9780691080871/symposium-on-infinite-dimensional-topology-am-69-volume-69"} ISBN:9780691080871
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Eells-FibringSpaces.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
          %p Textbook accounts:
          %ul
            %li#tDKP70
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tammo+tom+Dieck"}> Tammo tom Dieck
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klaus+Heiner+Kamps"} Klaus Heiner Kamps
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dieter+Puppe"} Dieter Puppe
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Homotopietheorie
                %strong 157
                Springer 1970 (
                %a{:href => "https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007/BFb0059721"}> doi:10.1007/BFb0059721
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alan+Hatcher"}> Alan Hatcher
                , from p. 61 on in
                %em Algebraic Topology
                (
                %a{:href => "http://www.math.cornell.edu/~hatcher/AT/ATpage.html"}> web
                )
          %p See also
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/R.+Schw%C3%A4nzl"}> R. Schwänzl
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/R.+Vogt"} R. Vogt
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Strong cofibrations and fibrations in enriched categories
            %li
              %p the textbooks on algebraic topology by Whitehead and Spanier.
            %li#May99
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+May"}> Peter May
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A+concise+course+in+algebraic+topology"} A concise course in algebraic topology
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.press.uchicago.edu/ucp/books/book/chicago/C/bo3777031.html"} ISBN: 9780226511832
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.math.uchicago.edu/~may/CONCISE/ConciseRevised.pdf"} pdf
            %li#Cutler20
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tyrone+Cutler"}> Tyrone Cutler
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Fibrations III – Locally trivial maps and bundles
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.math.uni-bielefeld.de/~tcutler/pdf/Fibrations%20III.pdf"} pdf
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/CutlerFibrationsIII.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
          %p Abstract analogues of Hurewicz fibrations can be found in
          %ul
            %li#Kamps72
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K.H.Kamps"}> K.H.Kamps
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Kan-Bedingungen und abstrakte Homotopietheorie
          %p summarised in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K.+H.+Kamps"}> K. H. Kamps
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tim+Porter"} Tim Porter
              %em Abstract Homotopy and Simple Homotopy Theory
              (
              %a{:href => "http://books.google.de/books?id=7JYKxInRMdAC&dq=Porter+Kamps&printsec=frontcover&source=bl&ots=uuyl_tIjs4&sig=Lt8I92xQBZ4DNKVXD0x76WkcxCE&hl=de&sa=X&oi=book_result&resnum=3&ct=result#PPP1,M1"}> GoogleBooks
              )
          %p and further developed in
          %ul
            %li#Williamson13
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Richard+Williamson"}> Richard Williamson
              ,
              %em Cylindrical model structures
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1304.0867"}> arXiv:1304.0867
              ,
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://rwilliamson-mathematics.info/cylindrical_model_structures.html"} web
          %p
            Discussion with an eye towards
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            is in
          %ul
            %li#Warren08
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Warren"}> Michael Warren
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Homotopy theoretic aspects of constructive type theory
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://mawarren.net/papers/phd.pdf"} PDF
